 ### [Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques](4646-reconstruction-and-compositing-techniques.md)
-Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques constitute the final stage of direct volume ray casting, where 3D volumetric data is projected into a 2D image by integrating sample contributions along viewing rays. This family solves the problem of correctly blending semi-transparent, overlapping density fields to produce physically plausible or semantically meaningful visualizations, handling tasks ranging from standard optical attenuation to differentiable training gradients and object-level importance modulation. What sets this family apart from its siblings is its focus on the mathematical integration of per-ray sample attributes (color, opacity, density) into a final pixel value, often leveraging specific mathematical forms (polynomials, kernels) or optimization strategies (early termination, packed layouts) rather than just acceleration structures or post-processing filters. See [Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques](4646-reconstruction-and-compositing-techniques.md).
