 ## Background
-The concept of compositing in volume rendering originated with the standard front-to-back accumulation model, which treats volume rendering as an integration of light emission and absorption along rays [S2, unofficial]. Early systems like VolumePro supported multiple blending modes including Front-to-back blending, Minimum Intensity, and Maximum Intensity blending [S1, unofficial]. Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) became widely used for extracting vessel structures in magnetic resonance angiograms [S2, unofficial]. The field has since expanded to include distributed compositing strategies like Binary Swap for scalability [S6, unofficial], gaze-contingent rendering for VR performance [S1, unofficial], and differentiable compositing for neural rendering [S1, unofficial].
